LANGUAGE MODELS

Second model available inside REPL, as ordinary calls. Use it only for work a
model must do: summarize, rewrite, judge, pull fields out of prose. Keep the
rest in Lisp. A call costs seconds and tokens: one or two per step, never in a
loop over a long list.

- (llm/complete "prompt") gives reply as string. It waits, so value is the text.

(llm/complete "Name three risks in this plan" :system "Be blunt." :max-tokens 200)
llm/complete-1: 84 words

- (llm/chat messages) gives reply as string, for multi-turn prompt. A message
  is (message :system "...") or (message :user "...") , an alist with "role"
  and "content", so a transcript as alist list is already a valid message list.
  Bare string in list counts as user message.

(llm/chat (list (message :system "Answer in one sentence.") (message :user q)))

- (llm/extract text shape) gives the data shape describes, as Lisp data. Model
  is constrained to shape: no parsing step, no prose to strip. Use it to turn a
  page, comment or mail into fields readable with assoc.

(llm/extract page (shape (title "the page title")
    (stars :integer)
    (state (:enum "open" "closed"))
    (links (:list :string))
    (author (:optional (:string "who wrote it")))))
llm/extract-1: alist, keys "title" "stars" "state" "links"

  Shape is alist of (key . field) , which (shape (name spec...) ...)
writes for you: no quote, no dotted pairs. Field is one of:
  :string, :number, :integer, :boolean, :any; plain string, meaning string
  field whose text says what it means;
(:string "what it means") and same for other scalars;
(:enum "a" "b") ; (:list field) ; (:optional field) , only field model
  may leave out; or nested alist, for nested object. Alist shape comes back
  as alist, (:list …) shape as list. Add :instructions "..." to say what to
  pull out of text.
  In shape, nested group is nested object, and (:list …) and (:optional …)
take a group too: (shape (items (:list (id :number) (title :string))))
is list of objects. Shape is literal, built at compile time, so computed enum
  needs alist written by hand.
- Every text argument: prompt, :system, :instructions, llm/extract text,
  message content. Each takes any value: list or alist goes in as printed form.
  No (string …) needed. Promise refused: await it first.
- (llm/providers) gives one row per reachable provider, as (provider default-model
  status) , status :ready or :no-api-key. First row is what a call with no
  :provider uses.

Options, all optional, every call takes them: :provider, keyword from
(llm/providers) · :model, string · :system · :max-tokens ·
:temperature · :reasoning-effort, one of :low, :medium, :high · :timeout in
milliseconds, default 60000. Failed or timed-out call raises: wrap in try when
you have a fallback.

Global llm-defaults is keyword list every call starts from. Option at call site
wins. (with-llm (option...) body...) extends it for one block, restores after:

(with-llm (:provider :openrouter :max-tokens 120)
  (summarize-each (head pages-1 3)))

Three macros over llm/complete carry the prompt for you; (doc 'summarize)
and the expansion are all there is to them. Each takes llm/complete options,
plus :words, which caps asked length and token budget.

- (summarize value :words 60 [option...]) gives summary text. Value's printed
  form goes into prompt, so it reads list of records as well as page of text.
- (summarize-each values :words 25 [option...]) gives list of summaries, one
  per element, in order. One call per element, sequential: slice first with
  (head x 5) rather than summarize hundreds of rows.
- (llm/answer question context :words 60 [option...]) gives answer drawn from
  context and nothing else. Question context does not answer comes back nil,
  not a guess. nil means "not in there", never a failed call: branch on it,
  do not echo it.

(setq brief (summarize acme/list-issues-1 :words 40))
brief: 39 words

(echo brief)

(setq owner (llm/answer "who is assigned to ENG-12?" acme/list-issues-1))
owner: "Nadia"

(echo (if owner owner "the issues do not say who is assigned"))

GOTCHAS

- Model call waits and can fail: the one call worth a try when you have a
  fallback, never worth a loop over a long list. llm/complete and llm/chat
  return text; llm/extract returns data.
